# A local function whose name is also a pub TYPE of std/list (`skipped`,
# `step`, `cursor`, `capped`, ...) loses to that type's constructor on the
# interpreter, and wins natively. The engines disagree.
#
# Run (from a directory holding only this file, since `kanso test` and
# `kanso play` take the whole directory as the module):
#   kanso test local_fn_loses_to_list_type_in_tests.kso
#   kanso play p.kso / kanso play p.kso --interp   (p.kso = this file with the
#   test replaced by  print "{(sum_all [1 2 3]).total}")
#
# Should: the test passes and both engines print 6. `skipped` is declared
# right here, and a local declaration outranks an import (chapter 11).
#
# Does: the test FAILS with
#   error[runtime]: `list/skipped` has no field `total`
# because `skipped` built std/list's `skipped` record (fields `burn` and
# `source`) instead of naming the function below. `kanso play p.kso` prints 6;
# `kanso play p.kso --interp` fails with the same error. Nothing at check
# time.
#
# In the diff port the call `skipped c (missed ...)` in patch/apply.kso failed
# this way under `kanso test patch` while every run of the program, native
# and interpreted, behaved, so the test was reporting the engine, not the
# code under test. The function is now called `passed_over`.
import "std/list"

type tally_
  total

fn skipped acc n
  tally_ (acc.total + n)

fn sum_all xs
  list/fold xs (tally_ 0) skipped

test_local_function_wins = (sum_all [1 2 3]).total == 6
